 "result": "This repo is a Flask-like framework (Tessel), not Django. The mechanism is in `src/tessel/views.py`:\n\n- **`VerbView`** (`views.py:138`) is the base class you subclass, defining `get()` and `post()`.\n  - **`VerbView.__init_subclass__`** (`views.py:165`) runs when you subclass. It scans for method names listed in `http_method_funcs` and sets `cls.methods` (e.g. `{\"GET\", \"POST\"}`), also merging in the bases' `methods`.\n- **`View.to_view_func(name, ...)`** (`views.py:86`, classmethod on base `View`, line 16) turns the class into a plain function.\n  - It builds an inner `view(**kwargs)` that instantiates the class (per request if `init_every_request` is true, otherwise once) and calls `self.run_view(**kwargs)` via `active_app.ensure_sync`.\n  - It attaches `view_class`, `methods`, and `provide_automatic_options` to the function, and applies any `cls.decorators`.\n  - You register the result with `app.attach_route_rule(path, view_func=CounterAPI.to_view_func(\"counter\"))`.\n- **`VerbView.run_view`** (`views.py:182`) does the per-method dispatch: `getattr(self, request.method.lower())`, falls back to `get` for HEAD if there is no `head` handler, and calls it with the URL kwargs.\n\nThe route's allowed methods come from `view.methods`, which was set by `__init_subclass__`.",
